  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (Through Wednesday)...\n\nToday is forecast to be the hottest day of our warm stretch with\nhighs across the CWA in the mid to upper 90s. Luckily for those \ntired of the heat, temperatures will start to cool down Friday. \n\nAhead of a shortwave progged to move through the region this\nafternoon and evening, elevated fire weather conditions are\nforecast. Temperatures topping 90 F in the afternoon combined \nwith widespread RH in the teens is likely across all locations\noutside of the mountains. Models have also been fairly consistent\nin depicting some breezy winds with gusts in the 15 to 25 mph \nrange this afternoon. The other potential concern for fire \nweather will be afternoon and evening thunderstorms capable of \nproducing strong erratic wind gusts. A few of these storms may \nalso occur with minimal precipitation, however with PWAT values \nwill be around 1\" so dry thunderstorms may be hard to come by. \n\nThe thunderstorms this afternoon and evening will be kicked off \nby a vort max riding between a stronger low to the NW and the \npesky ridge over the intermountain region south of us. Most of the\nconvection will initiate over the mountains of south-centeral \nMontana shortly after the noon hour and then push off to the east.\nDue to the \"dirty\" nature of the energy pushing through there \nwill be several rounds of storms that kick off of the terrain \nthrough the evening. In terms of other ingredients for the \nstorms, the PWAT values were already mentioned to be near 1\" \nwhich is plenty sufficient for storms in this region. ML and SB \nCAPE values will be the bare minimum with widespread values \nstaying under 1000 J/Kg, while isolated pockets of higher amounts\nare possible. Shear will be the same as we have seen over the past\nseveral days with a large area of 30 kts of bulk sheer and \nlocalized higher values. So this is a clear high shear and low \nCAPE event. Forecast soundings show a classic inverted-v type \nsounding with a lot of D-CAPE meaning that widespread strong winds\nlook likely. \n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, that low off the Pacific coast will\nbe pushed inland and through our area. This will bring cooler \ntemperatures and a chance for widespread precipitation. This setup\nlooks like it may also bring some winds due to the progression \nand orientation of the low, however the modeled low level surface \nwinds are not too strong as of now. The arrival time of this\nfeature has lagged some in recent model runs making this more of a\nSunday event and less of a Saturday event. The arrival time will\nmatter quite a bit though as some models show a good area of\ndiffluence out ahead of the low which could kick off some decent\nthunderstorms if this arrives during peak daytime heating. After \nthis weekend's low, the long range models keep the pattern fairly \nactive and progressive through the first week of September. WMR",
